#' Least Absolute Deviations Regression with Particle Swarm Optimization
#'
#' Solves least absolute deviations regression by minimizing the sum of
#' absolute residuals with the local best particle swarm optimizer.
#'
#' @param X A `matrix` that is the Design Matrix for the regression problem.
#'          Include a column of ones for an intercept.
#' @param y A `vec` containing the response values.
#'
#' @return
#' The estimated \eqn{\beta}{beta} parameter values for the least absolute
#' deviations fit.
#' @details
#' The objective is the sum of absolute residuals:
#' \deqn{f(\beta) = \sum_i \left\lvert y_i - \mathbf{x}_i^{T} \beta \right\rvert}{f(beta) = sum_i |y_i - x_i^T beta|}
#' Compared with the residual sum of squares, the absolute value leaves large
#' residuals with a constant rather than a growing influence, so the fit
#' tolerates heavy tailed errors and outlying responses.
#'
#' The price is that the objective has a kink at every \eqn{\beta}{beta} that
#' makes a residual zero, and for a design with \eqn{p}{p} columns a solution
#' sets \eqn{p}{p} of the residuals to zero. The minimum therefore sits on the
#' kinks, where no gradient exists.
#'
#' `LADFunction` in the source of this example supplies
#'
#' ```
#' double Evaluate(const arma::mat& beta);
#' ```
#'
#' and no `Gradient()`. That is ensmallen's arbitrary function contract.
#' Optimizers that need a derivative reject a class shaped this way at compile
#' time, so fewer optimizers accept it than accept the differentiable
#' objectives behind [lin_reg_lbfgs()] and [logistic_reg_lbfgs()].
#'
#' Particle swarm optimization scatters candidate parameter vectors across the
#' search space and moves each one toward the best point it has seen and the
#' best point its neighbors have seen. Only the shape of the starting matrix
#' reaches the optimizer, since the swarm is placed at random. Those draws
#' come from R's random number generator, so `set.seed()` makes a run
#' reproducible. Unseeded runs land at slightly different points, and the
#' spread widens as `X` gains columns.
#' @export
#' @examples
#' # Number of Points
#' n = 400
#'
#' # Select beta parameters
#' beta = c(2, -1.5, 0.8)
#'
#' # Number of Predictors (including intercept)
#' p = length(beta)
#'
#' # The swarm is initialized at random, so fix the seed
#' set.seed(512)
#'
#' # Generate predictors from a normal distribution
#' X_i = matrix(rnorm(n * (p - 1)), ncol = p - 1)
#'
#' # Add an intercept
#' X = cbind(1, X_i)
#'
#' # Generate responses with heavy tailed errors
#' y = X %*% beta + rt(n, df = 2)
#'
#' # Run optimization with particle swarm optimization
#' theta_hat = lad_reg_pso(X, y)
#'
#' # Compare against a derivative free search in base R
#' nm = optim(rep(0, p), function(b) sum(abs(y - X %*% b)),
#'            method = "Nelder-Mead",
#'            control = list(maxit = 20000, reltol = 1e-12))
#'
#' cbind(actual = beta, ensmallen = theta_hat, optim = nm$par)
#'
#' # Compare the objective each one reached
#' obj = function(b) sum(abs(y - X %*% b))
#' c(ensmallen = obj(theta_hat), optim = obj(nm$par))
lad_reg_pso <- function(X, y) {
    .Call(`_RcppEnsmallen_lad_reg_pso`, X, y)
}

#' Linear Regression with L-BFGS
#'
#' Solves the Linear Regression's Residual Sum of Squares using the L-BFGS
#' optimizer.
#'
#' @param X A `matrix` that is the Design Matrix for the regression problem.
#' @param y A `vec` containing the response values.
#'
#' @return
#' The estimated \eqn{\beta}{beta} parameter values for the linear regression.
#' @details
#' Consider the **Residual Sum of Squares**, also known as **RSS**, defined as:
#' \deqn{RSS\left( \beta \right) = \left( { \mathbf{y} - \mathbf{X} \beta } \right)^{T} \left( \mathbf{y} - \mathbf{X} \beta \right)}{RSS(beta) = (y - X*beta)^T * (y - X*beta)}
#' The objective function is defined as:
#' \deqn{f(\beta) = (y - X\beta)^2}{f(beta) = (y - X * beta)^2}
#' The gradient is defined as:
#' \deqn{\frac{\partial RSS}{\partial \beta} = -2 \mathbf{X}^{T} \left(\mathbf{y} - \mathbf{X} \beta \right)}{dRSS/dbeta = -2 X^T (y - X * beta)}
#' @export
#' @examples
#' # Number of Points
#' n = 1000
#'
#' # Select beta parameters
#' beta = c(-2, 1.5, 3, 8.2, 6.6)
#'
#' # Number of Predictors (including intercept)
#' p = length(beta)
#'
#' # Generate predictors from a normal distribution
#' X_i = matrix(rnorm(n), ncol = p - 1)
#'
#' # Add an intercept
#' X = cbind(1, X_i)
#'
#' # Generate y values
#' y = X%*%beta + rnorm(n / (p - 1))
#'
#' # Run optimization with lbfgs
#' theta_hat = lin_reg_lbfgs(X, y)
#'
#' # Verify parameters were recovered
#' cbind(actual = beta, estimated = theta_hat)
lin_reg_lbfgs <- function(X, y) {
    .Call(`_RcppEnsmallen_lin_reg_lbfgs`, X, y)
}

#' Logistic Regression with Adam
#'
#' Solves logistic regression with the mini-batch Adam optimizer by expressing
#' the negative log-likelihood as a separable objective function.
#'
#' @param X          A `matrix` that is the Design Matrix for the regression
#'                   problem. Include a column of ones for an intercept.
#' @param y          A `vec` of binary responses taking values 0 or 1.
#' @param batch_size An `integer` giving the number of observations per
#'                   mini-batch.
#' @param max_epochs An `integer` giving the number of complete passes to make
#'                   over the data.
#' @param step_size  A `double` giving the Adam step size. The default of 0.05
#'                   is deliberately larger than the 'ensmallen' Adam default
#'                   of 0.001; see Details.
#' @param verbose    A `logical` indicating whether to report the loss at the
#'                   end of each epoch through an `ens::PrintLoss` callback.
#'
#' @return
#' The estimated \eqn{\beta}{beta} parameter values for the logistic regression.
#' @details
#' The objective and gradient match those of [logistic_reg_lbfgs()] with
#' `lambda = 0`, but are evaluated one mini-batch at a time.
#'
#' The optimizer's `MaxIterations()` counts *data points visited*, not batches
#' and not epochs, so `max_epochs` is converted internally by multiplying by
#' `nrow(X)`.
#'
#' As of 'ensmallen' 3.10 the SGD family divides the step size by the batch
#' size, so a step size carried over from 'ensmallen' 2 will behave
#' differently unless `ENS_OLD_SEPARABLE_STEP_BEHAVIOR` is defined. This is why
#' `step_size` defaults to 0.05 rather than the 0.001 that `ens::Adam` itself
#' uses: with a batch size of 32 the effective step is `0.001 / 32`, roughly
#' `3e-5`, which leaves the optimizer far short of the optimum after 50 epochs.
#'
#' Adam shuffles the data between epochs. That shuffle draws on Armadillo's
#' random number generator, which 'RcppArmadillo' routes to R's own stream, so
#' the result *is* reproducible under [set.seed()]. The flip side is that each
#' call consumes random numbers from R's stream and therefore shifts any
#' [runif()] or [rnorm()] draws that follow it. Without an explicit seed,
#' successive calls start from different positions in the stream and so give
#' slightly different answers.
#' @export
#' @examples
#' # Number of Points
#' n = 5000
#'
#' # Select beta parameters
#' beta = c(-1, 0.7, -0.5, 1.2)
#'
#' # Number of Predictors (including intercept)
#' p = length(beta)
#'
#' # Generate the design matrix with an intercept
#' X = cbind(1, matrix(rnorm(n * (p - 1)), ncol = p - 1))
#'
#' # Generate binary responses
#' y = rbinom(n, size = 1, prob = 1 / (1 + exp(-X %*% beta)))
#'
#' # Run optimization with Adam
#' theta_hat = logistic_reg_adam(X, y)
#'
#' # Compare against the full-batch L-BFGS solution
#' cbind(actual = beta,
#'       adam = theta_hat,
#'       lbfgs = logistic_reg_lbfgs(X, y))
logistic_reg_adam <- function(X, y, batch_size = 32L, max_epochs = 50L, step_size = 0.05, verbose = FALSE) {
    .Call(`_RcppEnsmallen_logistic_reg_adam`, X, y, batch_size, max_epochs, step_size, verbose)
}

#' Logistic Regression with L-BFGS
#'
#' Solves logistic regression by minimizing the penalized negative
#' log-likelihood with the L-BFGS optimizer.
#'
#' @param X      A `matrix` that is the Design Matrix for the regression
#'               problem. Include a column of ones for an intercept.
#' @param y      A `vec` of binary responses taking values 0 or 1.
#' @param lambda A `double` giving the ridge penalty. The default of 0
#'               yields the maximum likelihood estimate.
#'
#' @return
#' The estimated \eqn{\beta}{beta} parameter values for the logistic regression.
#' @details
#' Writing \eqn{\eta = \mathbf{X} \beta}{eta = X * beta}, the objective is the
#' penalized negative log-likelihood:
#' \deqn{f(\beta) = \sum_i \left[ \log \left(1 + e^{\eta_i} \right) - y_i \eta_i \right] + \frac{\lambda}{2} \lVert \beta \rVert_2^2}{f(beta) = sum_i [log(1 + exp(eta_i)) - y_i * eta_i] + (lambda / 2) * ||beta||^2}
#' with gradient
#' \deqn{\nabla f(\beta) = \mathbf{X}^{T} \left( \sigma \left( \eta \right) - \mathbf{y} \right) + \lambda \beta}{grad f(beta) = X^T (sigma(eta) - y) + lambda * beta}
#' where \eqn{\sigma}{sigma} is the logistic function.
#'
#' The penalty is applied to every coefficient, including the intercept. This
#' keeps the example self-contained, since the function cannot know which
#' column of `X` holds the intercept. Production code normally leaves the
#' intercept unpenalized.
#'
#' Because the objective is convex, optimization starts from a vector of zeros
#' rather than a random point, which makes the result reproducible.
#' @export
#' @examples
#' # Number of Points
#' n = 1000
#'
#' # Select beta parameters
#' beta = c(-1, 0.7, -0.5, 1.2)
#'
#' # Number of Predictors (including intercept)
#' p = length(beta)
#'
#' # Generate predictors from a normal distribution
#' X_i = matrix(rnorm(n * (p - 1)), ncol = p - 1)
#'
#' # Add an intercept
#' X = cbind(1, X_i)
#'
#' # Generate binary responses
#' y = rbinom(n, size = 1, prob = 1 / (1 + exp(-X %*% beta)))
#'
#' # Run optimization with L-BFGS
#' theta_hat = logistic_reg_lbfgs(X, y)
#'
#' # Compare against glm()
#' cbind(actual = beta,
#'       ensmallen = theta_hat,
#'       glm = glm.fit(X, y, family = binomial())$coefficients)
logistic_reg_lbfgs <- function(X, y, lambda = 0.0) {
    .Call(`_RcppEnsmallen_logistic_reg_lbfgs`, X, y, lambda)
}

#' Logistic Regression with L-BFGS, Recording the Optimizer's Path
#'
#' Solves logistic regression as [logistic_reg_lbfgs()] does, and returns
#' every point the optimizer visited on the way rather than the fit alone.
#'
#' @param X      A `matrix` that is the Design Matrix for the regression
#'               problem. Include a column of ones for an intercept.
#' @param y      A `vec` of binary responses taking values 0 or 1.
#' @param lambda A `double` giving the ridge penalty. The default of 0
#'               yields the maximum likelihood estimate.
#'
#' @return
#' A `matrix` with one row per coefficient and one column per recorded point.
#' The first column is the starting point, a vector of zeros, and each column
#' after it holds the coordinates following one accepted step. The last column
#' is the fit, and matches what [logistic_reg_lbfgs()] returns for the same
#' arguments to within the optimizer's tolerance.
#'
#' Coefficients run down the rows and iterations run across the columns, so
#' two coordinates can be traced against each other with
#' `plot(path[2, ], path[4, ])`.
#' @details
#' The objective, its gradient, the starting point and the optimizer settings
#' are those of [logistic_reg_lbfgs()], which documents them. The only
#' addition is an `ens::L_BFGS` callback that copies the coordinates into a
#' matrix each time the optimizer moves.
#'
#' A callback in 'ensmallen' is a plain class that defines whichever of the
#' documented hooks it needs; the library detects them at compile time. This
#' one uses `BeginOptimization()` for the starting point, `StepTaken()` for
#' each accepted step, and `EndOptimization()` for the point the run finished
#' on. Callbacks are passed as trailing arguments to `Optimize()`.
#'
#' The column count is not fixed. L-BFGS stops once the gradient norm falls
#' below its tolerance or the objective stops improving, so the number of
#' steps depends on the data. A point that repeats the previous one is not
#' recorded twice.
#' @export
#' @examples
#' # Number of Points
#' n = 1000
#'
#' # Select beta parameters
#' beta = c(-1, 0.7, -0.5, 1.2)
#'
#' # Number of Predictors (including intercept)
#' p = length(beta)
#'
#' # Generate predictors from a normal distribution
#' X_i = matrix(rnorm(n * (p - 1)), ncol = p - 1)
#'
#' # Add an intercept
#' X = cbind(1, X_i)
#'
#' # Generate binary responses
#' y = rbinom(n, size = 1, prob = 1 / (1 + exp(-X %*% beta)))
#'
#' # Record the path L-BFGS takes
#' path = logistic_reg_lbfgs_path(X, y)
#'
#' # One row per coefficient, one column per recorded point
#' dim(path)
#'
#' # The last column is the fit
#' cbind(actual = beta,
#'       path = path[, ncol(path)],
#'       lbfgs = drop(logistic_reg_lbfgs(X, y)))
#'
#' # Trace two coefficients against each other
#' plot(path[2, ], path[4, ], type = "b", pch = 16,
#'      xlab = "beta 2", ylab = "beta 4",
#'      main = "L-BFGS path through two coordinates")
#'
#' # Mark where the run started and where it ended
#' points(path[2, 1], path[4, 1], col = "blue", pch = 16, cex = 1.5)
#' points(path[2, ncol(path)], path[4, ncol(path)],
#'        col = "red", pch = 16, cex = 1.5)
logistic_reg_lbfgs_path <- function(X, y, lambda = 0.0) {
    .Call(`_RcppEnsmallen_logistic_reg_lbfgs_path`, X, y, lambda)
}

#' Non-Negative Least Squares with the Augmented Lagrangian
#'
#' Solves least squares subject to every coefficient being non-negative with
#' the augmented Lagrangian optimizer.
#'
#' @param X A `matrix` that is the Design Matrix for the regression problem.
#'          Every column is constrained, so do not include a column of ones
#'          for an intercept.
#' @param y A `vec` of continuous responses.
#'
#' @return
#' The estimated \eqn{\beta}{beta} parameter values, each of them
#' non-negative.
#' @details
#' The objective is the residual sum of squares
#' \deqn{f(\beta) = \lVert \mathbf{y} - \mathbf{X} \beta \rVert_2^2}{f(beta) = ||y - X * beta||^2}
#' with gradient
#' \deqn{\nabla f(\beta) = -2 \mathbf{X}^{T} \left( \mathbf{y} - \mathbf{X} \beta \right)}{grad f(beta) = -2 * X^T (y - X * beta)}
#' minimized subject to \eqn{\beta_j \ge 0}{beta_j >= 0} for every column
#' \eqn{j}{j} of `X`.
#'
#' `ens::AugLagrangian` calls for the constrained function contract, which
#' extends the `Evaluate()` and `Gradient()` pair of a differentiable function
#' with `NumConstraints()`, `EvaluateConstraint()` and `GradientConstraint()`.
#' Every constraint is an equality, \eqn{c_i(\beta) = 0}{c_i(beta) = 0}, and a
#' bound has to be recast as a residual that is zero while the bound holds.
#' Here that residual is \eqn{\min(0, \beta_j)}{min(0, beta_j)}, one per
#' coefficient.
#'
#' The bound is imposed through a penalty rather than by projection, so a
#' coefficient resting on the boundary is returned as a small number of order
#' 1e-10 rather than as exactly zero, and that number can be slightly
#' negative. Round the result before comparing it with zero.
#'
#' Optimization starts from a vector of zeros, which satisfies every
#' constraint and makes the result reproducible.
#' @export
#' @examples
#' # Number of Points
#' n = 100
#'
#' # Select beta parameters, two of which are exactly zero
#' beta = c(2, 0, 1.5, 0)
#'
#' # Number of Predictors
#' p = length(beta)
#'
#' set.seed(24)
#'
#' # Generate predictors from a normal distribution
#' X = matrix(rnorm(n * p), ncol = p)
#'
#' # Generate continuous responses
#' y = as.vector(X %*% beta + rnorm(n))
#'
#' # The unconstrained least squares fit lands outside the feasible region
#' ols = qr.solve(X, y)
#' min(ols)
#'
#' # Run optimization with the augmented Lagrangian
#' beta_hat = nnls_auglag(X, y)
#'
#' # Compare against optim() with a lower bound of zero
#' rss = function(b) sum((y - X %*% b)^2)
#' box = optim(rep(0, p), rss, method = "L-BFGS-B", lower = rep(0, p))
#'
#' cbind(actual = beta,
#'       ols = ols,
#'       ensmallen = beta_hat,
#'       optim = box$par)
nnls_auglag <- function(X, y) {
    .Call(`_RcppEnsmallen_nnls_auglag`, X, y)
}

